                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I don't play platformers. Period.  Last time I grinded one was probably back in the '80s.  Yes, I'm old, really old.  Just playing a vanilla platformer is challenging for me.  Handling the dual 'dimension' switch...ok.. Throw in bullet dodging, strobing lights, dark outs, fluctuating gravity (water), and then upside down mechanics, I surrender.  After about an hour and half, my head was swimming and I had to put the game down.  So I'm not evaluating how good this is among other challenging platformers. \r\n\r\nWhat I like is the 'originality' of the story premise.  It is maybe uncomfortable and intrusive for some.  Still, it ties the emotional disorder to the chaotic disorder of the game mechanics.  Nicely done.  I think it deserves a thumbs up for just the experience, after all it's free.  Price of admission is two tylenols. \r\n\r\nI also respect the game designer(s) who wanted to share the personal and painful experience.",
